= 1H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1H}

Let $g$ generate the <cyclic group> of nonzero residues, whose order $p-1=2^{2^n}$ is a power of two. Write $a=g^j$. Its <multiplicative order> is $(p-1)/\gcd(j,p-1)$, so it is a <primitive root> precisely when $j$ is odd. The <quadratic residues> are exactly the even powers of $g$. Thus **the primitive roots are precisely the quadratic nonresidues**, with nonzero <residue> classes understood.

For $n\geq1$, $p\equiv1\pmod4$, so <quadratic reciprocity> gives $(7/p)=(p/7)$. Since $2^3\equiv1\pmod7$ and $2^n$ alternates between $1$ and $2$ modulo three, $p$ is respectively $3$ or $5$ modulo seven. The nonzero squares modulo seven are $1,2,4$. Consequently $(7/p)=-1$, and
$$
\boxed{7\text{ is a primitive root modulo every Fermat prime }2^{2^n}+1\text{ with }n\geq1.}
$$
There is a genuine endpoint exception if the printed form permits $n=0$: then $p=3$ and $7\equiv1\pmod3$, which has order one and is not primitive. The first equivalence remains true for $p=3$, but the final assertion requires excluding that prime.

= 2F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= i
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Liouville approximation theorem> states that if $\alpha$ is a real irrational <algebraic number> of degree $d$, there is a constant $C(\alpha)>0$ such that, for all integers $p,q$ with $q\ne0$,
$$
\boxed{\left|\alpha-\frac pq\right|\geq\frac{C(\alpha)}{|q|^d}.}
$$
Reduction of the fraction is not necessary: an unreduced denominator only weakens this bound. More generally the same type of bound holds for an <algebraic number> of degree $d$ whenever $p/q\ne\alpha$. The exception is essential for a rational $\alpha$, since an exact rational representation gives zero error. A <rational number> of degree one obeys the bound directly: if $\alpha=a/b$ in lowest terms and $p/q\ne\alpha$, then $|\alpha-p/q|=|aq-bp|/(|bq|)\geq1/(|bq|)$.

= ii
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $r=p/q$. If $r\notin[1,2]$, then $|\sqrt2-r|\geq\sqrt2-1>1/4\geq1/(4q^2)$. If $1\leq r\leq2$, <irrationality of the square root of two> implies that the <integer> $2q^2-p^2$ is nonzero. Factoring a difference of squares therefore gives
$$
\left|\sqrt2-\frac pq\right|
=\frac{|2q^2-p^2|}{q^2(\sqrt2+p/q)}
\geq\frac1{q^2(\sqrt2+2)}
>\frac1{4q^2}.
$$
Thus the requested **bound holds for every <integer> $p$ and every nonzero <integer> $q$**, without using the <Liouville approximation theorem>. For <completeness>, irrationality follows because $p^2=2q^2$ in lowest terms would make $p$ even and then $q$ even, contradicting <coprimality>.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry of Group Actions}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Use the curvature-minus-one <Poincare disc model>, whose <Riemannian metric> is
$$
ds^2=\frac{4(dx^2+dy^2)}{(1-x^2-y^2)^2}.
$$
Its <geodesics> are Euclidean diameters and arcs of circles meeting the boundary circle orthogonally. The <hyperbolic area> of a measurable region $D$ is
$$
\operatorname{Area}(D)=\int_D\frac4{(1-x^2-y^2)^2}\,dx\,dy.
$$
An <isometry> can move the center of a circle to the origin. Its hyperbolic radius $r$ and Euclidean radius $\rho$ then satisfy $r=\int_0^\rho2\,du/(1-u^2)=2\operatorname{artanh}\rho$, so $\rho=\tanh(r/2)$. Integrating the <area element of a surface> in <polar coordinates> yields
$$
A(r)=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\rho\frac{4u}{(1-u^2)^2}\,du\,d\theta
=\frac{4\pi\rho^2}{1-\rho^2}
=\boxed{2\pi(\cosh r-1)}.
$$
Integrating the line element around the circle gives
$$
C(r)=\frac{4\pi\rho}{1-\rho^2}=\boxed{2\pi\sinh r},
\qquad A'(r)=2\pi\sinh r=C(r).
$$
A geometric definition of <pi> valid in this geometry is
$$
\boxed{\pi=\lim_{r\downarrow0}\frac{C(r)}{2r}
=\lim_{r\downarrow0}\frac{A(r)}{r^2}.}
$$
Indeed $\sinh r=r+O(r^3)$ and $\cosh r=1+r^2/2+O(r^4)$. The circumference-to-diameter ratio at a fixed positive radius is not constant, so the limiting qualification is necessary.

= 4J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=4J}

A <binary linear code> of length $n$ is a <vector subspace> $C\subseteq\mathbb F_2^n$. The <Hamming weight> of a word counts its nonzero coordinates, and the code weight is $w(C)=\min_{0\ne c\in C}\operatorname{wt}(c)$; for a <linear code> this equals its <minimum distance>. One may assign infinite minimum weight to the zero code when needed.

For length-$n$ codes with $C_2\subseteq C_1$, the <bar product of binary linear codes> is
$$
C_1|C_2=\{(u,u+v):u\in C_1,\ v\in C_2\}\subseteq\mathbb F_2^{2n}.
$$
It is a <linear code> because this map from $C_1\oplus C_2$ is linear and injective. For a nonzero <codeword>, if $v=0$ then $u\ne0$ and its weight is $2\operatorname{wt}(u)\geq2w(C_1)$. If $v\ne0$, every nonzero coordinate of $v$ makes exactly one of the corresponding coordinates of $u,u+v$ nonzero; hence
$$
\operatorname{wt}(u)+\operatorname{wt}(u+v)\geq\operatorname{wt}(v)\geq w(C_2).
$$
Taking the minimum gives
$$
\boxed{w(C_1|C_2)\geq\min\{2w(C_1),w(C_2)\}.}
$$
In fact equality holds when the codes are nonzero: minimum-weight words $(u,u)$ and $(0,v)$ attain the two candidate weights.

= 5I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=5I}

Each `scan()` command reads a numeric vector from the entered data: the first creates the nine counts, and the second their nine predictor values. The last command fits a <generalized linear model> with the <Poisson distribution> and its default <log link>, including an intercept, then prints a summary. Thus the model is <independent> counts with means $\mu_j$ satisfying
$$
\log\mu_j=b_0+b_1i_j,
\qquad \widehat\mu_j=\exp(1.363+0.3106i_j).
$$
The coefficients are <maximum likelihood estimates>, and the reported standard errors come from the inverse fitted <Fisher information matrix>. Increasing the predictor by one multiplies the fitted mean by $e^{0.3106}\simeq1.364$, about a $36.4\%$ increase. The intercept corresponds to a mean $e^{1.363}\simeq3.91$ at predictor zero, outside the observed range. Approximate coefficient-to-standard-error ratios are $6.17$ and $8.13$; the slope is strongly different from zero under the assumed model. A $95\%$ <Wald confidence interval> for the slope is about $[0.236,0.385]$, or $[1.266,1.470]$ for the multiplicative change.

The <deviance> is twice the log-likelihood advantage of the <saturated model> over the fitted model. For these counts it is
$$
D=2\sum_{j=1}^9\left[n_j\log\frac{n_j}{\widehat\mu_j}-(n_j-\widehat\mu_j)\right],
$$
with the logarithmic term interpreted as zero when a count is zero. The <saturated model> has nine freely fitted means; the fitted model has two <independent> regression coefficients, so the residual <degrees of freedom> are **$9-2=7$**.

Under an adequate Poisson model and the usual large-sample approximation, $D$ is compared with $\chi^2_7$. The reported $13.218$ is rather large relative to seven, but below the $5\%$ upper critical value about $14.07$; its approximate upper-tail <probability> is $0.067$. Thus there is some indication of lack of fit or <overdispersion>, but not a rejection at the $5\%$ level on this diagnostic. The deviance-to-d.f. ratio is about $1.89$; it does not by itself prove an overdispersed model, and the chi-square approximation is imperfect for small counts.

There is one inconsistency in the supplied numerical output. Independently solving the Poisson score equations for the displayed nine counts gives $\widehat b_0=1.363454$, $\widehat b_1=0.310563$ and deviance $13.217971$, agreeing with the printed estimates and deviance. But $[X^{\mathsf T}\operatorname{diag}(\widehat\mu)X]^{-1}$ gives standard errors approximately $0.221295$ and $0.030850$. In particular the printed slope standard error $0.0382$ is not the standard Poisson-model value for these data. The interpretations above describe the supplied numbers; the directly recomputed slope ratio is $10.07$ and its normal-approximation $95\%$ interval is about $[0.2501,0.3710]$. The conclusion of a strong increasing trend is unchanged.

= 6E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= a
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the formal <infinitesimal generator> to the molecule count: its increment is $+1$ at rate $\lambda$ and $-2$ at rate $\beta x^2$. Thus the exact first-moment identity for the specified generator is
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\langle x\rangle=\lambda-2\beta\langle x^2\rangle
=\lambda-2\beta\big(\langle x\rangle^2+\operatorname{Var}x\big).}
$$
Neglecting fluctuations gives the <mean-field approximation> $\dot m=\lambda-2\beta m^2$, so the positive steady mean is
$$
\boxed{m=\langle x\rangle\simeq\sqrt{\lambda/(2\beta)}.}
$$
There is a physical boundary qualification: the printed loss propensity is positive at $x=1$ and would produce a negative molecule count. Consequently it does not by itself define a molecule-count chain on all nonnegative integers. If losses are forbidden below two, the exact loss moment becomes $2\beta E[x^2\mathbf1_{\{x\geq2\}}]$; a mass-action two-molecule propensity instead uses $x(x-1)$. The calculation requested here uses the printed formal generator and its large-copy-number approximation, where such boundary corrections are negligible.

= b
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $J_+=\lambda$ and $J_-=2\beta m^2$ be the production and loss fluxes, counted in molecules per unit time. At the <steady state>, both equal $J=\lambda$. The restoring <elasticity> and turnover lifetime are
$$
\boxed{H=\frac{d\log J_-}{d\log m}-\frac{d\log J_+}{d\log m}=2,
\qquad\tau=\frac mJ=\frac m\lambda=\frac1{2\beta m}.}
$$
Flux-weighting the magnitudes of the reaction steps, one for production and two for loss, gives
$$
\boxed{\langle r\rangle=\frac{J_+\cdot1+J_-\cdot2}{J_++J_-}=\frac32.}
$$
This is an average over molecular fluxes, as requested, not an average over reaction-event counts; those are different when a reaction removes two molecules at once.

= c
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a stable one-species <linear noise approximation>, the <normalized stationary fluctuation-dissipation relation for a reaction network> becomes
$$
\boxed{\eta=\frac{\operatorname{Var}x}{m^2}
=\frac{\langle r\rangle}{Hm}.}
$$
To see the factors explicitly, the linearized restoring rate is $a=H/\tau=4\beta m$, while the chemical noise intensity is the rate-weighted squared step size
$$
B=\lambda\cdot1^2+\beta m^2\cdot(-2)^2=3\lambda.
$$
Stationarity of the <variance> gives $2a\operatorname{Var}x=B$. Using $\lambda=2\beta m^2$ therefore yields
$$
\operatorname{Var}x=\frac{3m}{4},\qquad
\boxed{\eta=\frac3{4m}=\frac3{4\langle x\rangle}.}
$$
This is the stationary fluctuation result within the stated negligible-fluctuation/linear-noise approximation, not an exact identity for the boundary-corrected discrete chemistry.

= 7B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=7B}

A <fixed point> $x_0$ is <Lyapunov stable> if for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $\delta>0$ such that solutions starting within $\delta$ of $x_0$ stay within $\varepsilon$ for all forward times. It has <quasi-asymptotic stability> if some neighborhood of $x_0$ consists of initial points whose solutions converge to $x_0$. <Asymptotic stability> requires both properties.

Choose the <Lyapunov function>
$$
\boxed{V(x,y)=\frac{x^6}{3}+y^2.}
$$
It vanishes only at the origin, is positive elsewhere, and has compact sublevel sets. The vector field is polynomial, hence locally Lipschitz. Along a solution,
$$
\dot V=2x^5(-y-x^3)+2yx^5=-2x^8\leq0.
$$
A small sublevel set is forward invariant and can be fitted inside any prescribed neighborhood, proving <Lyapunov stability>; boundedness also prevents finite-time escape.

For attraction, a bounded forward solution has a nonempty compact limit set, and $V$ decreases to a limit. Every point in that limit set has that same $V$ value. Continuous dependence of solutions makes the limit set forward invariant, so $\dot V=0$ along every trajectory in it. Such a trajectory must have $x=0$ at all times. But then $\dot x=-y$, forcing $y=0$. Thus the only possible limit point is the origin, and every solution converges there. This verifies the invariant-set condition behind the <LaSalle invariance principle>, rather than incorrectly treating $\dot V$ as strictly negative at every nonzero point. The origin is therefore **asymptotically stable**, indeed globally attracting.

= 8A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=8A}

Fix a <Hankel contour> with a positive-radius circle around zero and a branch $-\pi<\arg t<\pi$, traversing the lower bank inwards and the upper bank outwards. Write its numerator as $I(z)=\int_Ht^{z-1}e^t\,dt$. For fixed circle radius, $I$ is an <entire function> of $z$: the finite contour pieces avoid zero, and the tails converge uniformly on compact $z$ sets by exponential decay, also after differentiation with respect to $z$.

At $z=-n$, the two straight-bank contributions cancel because $t^{-n-1}e^t$ is single-valued. The remaining circle is positively oriented, so the <residue theorem> and the <exponential series> give
$$
I(-n)=2\pi i\operatorname{Res}_{t=0}(t^{-n-1}e^t)=\frac{2\pi i}{n!}.
$$
Since $\sin(\pi z)=(-1)^n\pi(z+n)+O((z+n)^3)$, the quotient has the <simple pole>
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=-n}\Gamma(z)=\frac{(-1)^n}{n!},\qquad n\geq0.}
$$
For <cancellation of positive-integer gamma singularities on a Hankel contour>, at a positive <integer> $n$, the integrand $t^{n-1}e^t$ is entire and its bank contributions again cancel; its circular integral is zero. Hence $I(n)=0$. The numerator is analytic near $n$, while the denominator has a simple zero with nonzero derivative. Factoring $I(z)=(z-n)J(z)$ therefore cancels that zero, proving **there is no <pole> at a positive <integer>** using only this representation. Cancellation of the numerator, not merely the zero of the sine, decides the issue. One can also shrink the contour for $\Re z>0$, obtaining $I(z)=2i\sin(\pi z)\int_0^\infty u^{z-1}e^{-u}\,du$ and hence the finite value $(n-1)!$.

= 9C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=9C}

The components in <Euler equations for a torque-free rigid body> are evaluated in the body-fixed principal-axis frame, with $L_i=I_i\omega_i$. In an inertial space frame the <angular momentum> vector is constant; its components in the rotating body frame need not be.

Assume $\Omega\ne0$ and distinct positive <principal moments of inertia>. Write $\omega=(\Omega+\delta_1,\delta_2,\delta_3)$. To first order,
$$
\dot\delta_1=0,\qquad
\dot\delta_2=\frac{I_3-I_1}{I_2}\Omega\delta_3,
\qquad\dot\delta_3=\frac{I_1-I_2}{I_3}\Omega\delta_2,
$$
so
$$
\ddot\delta_2=\Omega^2\frac{(I_3-I_1)(I_1-I_2)}{I_2I_3}\delta_2.
$$
The coefficient is positive when $I_1$ lies between the other moments, giving an exponentially growing mode and instability. When $I_1$ is an extreme moment, it is negative and the transverse perturbations oscillate.

To establish actual nonlinear <Lyapunov stability> in the latter case, use the conserved <kinetic energy> $E=\frac12\sum I_j\omega_j^2$ and squared <angular momentum> $L^2=\sum I_j^2\omega_j^2$. Their combination is
$$
L^2-2I_1E=I_2(I_2-I_1)\omega_2^2+I_3(I_3-I_1)\omega_3^2.
$$
For an extreme $I_1$ its two coefficients have the same sign. Conservation controls both transverse components by their initially small values, while conservation of $E$ controls $\omega_1^2$ near $\Omega^2$. <Continuity> preserves its initial sign for sufficiently small perturbations. Thus **nonzero steady rotation is stable exactly about the largest or smallest principal moment**, the <intermediate axis theorem>. It is not asymptotically stable, since <energy> is conserved. If $\Omega=0$, <energy> makes the zero angular-velocity state stable for every ordering; the nonzero-rotation assumption is essential to the stated equivalence.

= 10D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= a
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let the surface be at $R$ with vanishing external <pressure>. Multiply <hydrostatic equilibrium> by $4\pi r^3$ and integrate:
$$
\int_0^R4\pi r^3P'(r)\,dr
=[4\pi r^3P]_0^R-3\int_0^R4\pi r^2P\,dr
=-3\langle P\rangle V.
$$
The other side is $-\int_0^R4\pi Gm(r)\rho(r)r\,dr=-\int_0^M Gm/r(m)\,dm$. Building the star one shell at a time identifies this as its negative <gravitational potential energy> $E_{\rm grav}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{3\langle P\rangle V=-E_{\rm grav}.}
$$
If the star has nonzero surface <pressure> $P_s$, the integration instead gives $3\langle P\rangle V-3P_sV=-E_{\rm grav}$; the requested form assumes $P_s=0$.

= b
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Within the prescribed uniform-density approximation, $n=3M/(4\pi m_pR^3)$. Treating the given approximate <pressure> coefficient as one, the <degeneracy pressure> and specified kinetic-energy relation give
$$
E_{\rm kin}=\frac32\frac{h^2}{m_e}n^{5/3}\frac{4\pi R^3}{3}
=\frac{\alpha}{R^2},
\qquad\alpha=\frac32\frac{h^2}{m_e}\left(\frac3{4\pi}\right)^{2/3}
\left(\frac M{m_p}\right)^{5/3}.
$$
For a uniform sphere, $m(r)=M(r/R)^3$, so shell assembly gives
$$
E_{\rm grav}=-\int_0^R\frac{Gm(r)}r\,dm(r)=-\frac{3GM^2}{5R}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{E_{\rm total}(R)=\frac\alpha{R^2}-\frac\beta R,
\qquad\beta=\frac35GM^2.}
$$
At fixed mass, this <energy> has its minimum at $R_*=2\alpha/\beta$, with $E''(R_*)=2\alpha/R_*^4>0$. Expansion beyond this radius raises the <energy>, while strong compression costs positive <energy> growing as $R^{-2}$, faster than the attractive $R^{-1}$ term. This explains radial stability of the model <white dwarf>. The numerical coefficient in $\alpha$ inherits the approximate <pressure> law; a relativistic degeneracy law would change this stability argument.

= 11F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= i
{parent=11f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Baire category theorem> says that in a nonempty complete <metric space>, a <countable> intersection of open dense subsets is dense. Equivalently, such a space cannot be a <countable> union of <nowhere dense sets>: a set is <nowhere dense> when the interior of its closure is empty. Indeed the complements of those closures are open dense; their intersection is dense and in particular nonempty, so a <countable> union of the original sets cannot exhaust the space. The same argument shows that the complement of such a union is dense.

= ii
{parent=11f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

First interpret the decimal expansions as fractional expansions $0.d_1d_2\cdots$, with every $d_j\in\{4,5\}$. The resulting set $X_0$ is compact: a diagonal subsequence argument selects successively stabilized digits from any sequence in $X_0$, and the tail bound $\sum_{j>m}9\cdot10^{-j}=10^{-m}$ proves convergence to the corresponding expansion.

It has empty interior. At level $m$ its points lie in $2^m$ decimal intervals, each of length $10^{-m}$, whose total length is $(1/5)^m$. An interval of positive length could not lie in all these covers. Thus $X_0$ is closed and <nowhere dense>. Each translate $X_0+q$ is also <nowhere dense>, and the <rational numbers> are <countable>. The <Baire category theorem> applied to $\mathbb R$ yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbb R\setminus\bigcup_{q\in\mathbb Q}(X_0+q)\ne\varnothing.}
$$
Any point in this complement has the required property. If the printed description also permits <integer> digits before the decimal point, there are only countably many such <integer> parts, and negative expansions merely add reflected translates. This makes $X$ a <countable> union of translates or reflections of $X_0$. The same countable-union argument therefore still proves that some real $t$ cannot be written as $x+q$.

= 12J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=12J}

A <linear feedback shift register> of length $d$ has binary state $(x_r,\ldots,x_{r+d-1})$ and update
$$
f(v_0,\ldots,v_{d-1})=(v_1,\ldots,v_{d-1},\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}a_jv_j).
$$
Its output obeys $x_{r+d}=\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}a_jx_{r+j}$ over $\mathbb F_2$. Since the state space is finite, its trajectory consists of a transient of length $M$ followed by a cycle of length $N\geq1$, and the output satisfies $x_{r+N}=x_r$ for $r\geq M$.

If the trajectory never reaches zero, all its $M+N$ distinct transient and cyclic states are nonzero; hence $M+N\leq2^d-1$. If it reaches zero, let $M$ be its first zero-state time. The vectors $v,fv,\ldots,f^{M-1}v$ are <linearly independent>: in a dependence, apply $f^{M-1-j}$ where $j$ is the smallest index with a nonzero coefficient; every later term vanishes and the remaining term is $f^{M-1}v\ne0$. Therefore $M\leq d$. The zero cycle has $N=1$, and $d+1\leq2^d-1$ for $d\geq2$. This proves the requested bound for **all $d\geq2$**, and for every invertible register.

The unrestricted printed assertion has one exception: at $d=1$, the zero-feedback register with initial state one outputs $1,0,0,\ldots$. It requires $M\geq1,N\geq1$, so $M+N\geq2>2^1-1$. The universally valid finite-state bound is $M+N\leq2^d$. A register with feedback from its oldest bit is invertible, in which case a nonzero state never reaches zero and the sharper bound holds already with $M=0$.

To recover an unknown register from a known plaintext/ciphertext keystream, the <Berlekamp-Massey algorithm> finds a shortest <linear recurrence> consistent with the observed bits. Use a connection polynomial $C(z)=1+\sum_{j=1}^LC_jz^j$, so the recurrence discrepancy at index $r$ is $\delta=x_r+\sum_{j=1}^LC_jx_{r-j}$. Initialize $C=B=1,L=0,b=1,m=1$. Process the bits in order. If $\delta=0$, increment $m$. If $\delta\ne0$, save $T=C$ and replace
$$
C\longleftarrow C+(\delta/b)z^mB.
$$
If $2L\leq r$, also replace $L$ by $r+1-L$, set $B=T,b=\delta,m=1$; otherwise retain $L,B,b$ and increment $m$. Arithmetic is binary, so every nonzero discrepancy equals one. The stored polynomial $B$ last failed at an earlier index with discrepancy $b$; its shifted contribution cancels the new failure without changing previous successful discrepancies.

Here is the <minimum recurrence length after a new discrepancy> argument behind the update, not just its name. Suppose a span-$L$ recurrence $C$ succeeds through index $r-1$ but fails at $r$. If another polynomial $D$ of span $L'$ succeeded through $r$ and $L+L'\leq r$, compute the <convolution> $(CD)x$ at $r$ in two ways. Applying $D$ first gives zero, since all indices $r-j$ used by $C$ lie at least at $L'$. Applying $C$ first gives its nonzero discrepancy at $r$, since its earlier discrepancies used by $D$ vanish and $D_0=1$. This contradiction proves $L'\geq r+1-L$. The shifted-polynomial update attains the larger of the existing minimum length and this new lower bound; induction therefore gives the shortest recurrence for every prefix. Once at least twice the true linear complexity has been observed, this recurrence determines the whole stream: the difference from a true degree-$d$ recurrent stream satisfies a recurrence of degree at most $d+L$, and a prefix of that many zeros forces every later value to be zero. Without any bound on complexity, a finite observed prefix cannot exclude a longer register that diverges later; the breaking claim uses the finite-register hypothesis and adequate known data.

Finally each of the three input streams is eventually periodic. Let $M$ be the maximum of their transient lengths and $N$ a common multiple of their periods. Their joint triple, and hence the bit chosen by the specified rule, repeats after $N$ once $r\geq M$. Therefore
$$
k_{r+M+N}=k_{r+M}\qquad(r\geq0).
$$
This itself is a <linear recurrence> of length $M+N$, so it constructs a <linear feedback shift register> producing the selected stream from its first $M+N$ bits. No closure theorem is being assumed: eventual periodicity and this explicit recurrence prove **the combined stream is also an LFSR stream**, even though the combining rule is nonlinear in its three inputs.

= 13E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= a
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose the <nondimensionalization>
$$
\boxed{t=\beta_u\tau,\qquad s=x\sqrt{\beta_u/D_u},\qquad
Q=\frac{\beta_v}{\beta_u},\quad P=\frac{D_v\beta_u}{D_u\beta_v}.}
$$
The <chain rule> gives $\partial_\tau=\beta_u\partial_t$ and $\partial_x^2=(\beta_u/D_u)\partial_s^2$. Dividing the first equation by $\beta_u$ and the second by $\beta_v$ gives the required scaled <reaction-diffusion system>. Positive degradation and <diffusion> constants are assumed.

= b
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A nonzero spatially constant <steady state> satisfies $v=u$ and $v=u^2$, hence $(u,v)=(1,1)$. The first derivatives of $u^2/v-u$ there are $1,-1$, while those of $Q(u^2-v)$ are $2Q,-Q$. Thus
$$
\boxed{J=\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\2Q&-Q\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\operatorname{tr}J=1-Q,\quad\det J=Q.}
$$
Since $Q>0$, both <eigenvalues> have strictly negative real part exactly when **$Q>1$**. For $0<Q<1$ the equilibrium is unstable. At $Q=1$ its linear <eigenvalues> are $\pm i$; this is the marginal boundary and does not give <asymptotic stability> from <linearization>. The positive-trace criterion in the hint tests positive real parts; stability requires applying it to $-J$.

= c
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set $u=1+\widehat u(t)\cos(s/L)$ and $v=1+\widehat v(t)\cos(s/L)$ and retain linear terms. With $k=1/L$, the <Fourier mode> has second derivative $-k^2\cos(ks)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\begin{pmatrix}\widehat u\\\widehat v\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1-k^2&-1\\2Q&-Q(1+Pk^2)\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\widehat u\\\widehat v\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The trace and <determinant> of this <linearization> are
$$
T(k)=1-Q-(1+QP)k^2,
\qquad D(k)=Q[1+(1-P)k^2+Pk^4].
$$

= d
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Assume $Q>1$, so $T(k)<0$ for every <wavenumber>. A <Turing instability> therefore occurs exactly when $D(k)<0$ for some $k$. Put $z=k^2\geq0$. The upward quadratic $1+(1-P)z+Pz^2$ has a negative minimum on this half-line precisely when
$$
P>1,\qquad(P-1)^2>4P.
$$
Combining these gives
$$
\boxed{P>3+2\sqrt2.}
$$
The unstable band is $z_-<k^2<z_+$, where
$$
z_\pm=\frac{P-1\pm\sqrt{(P-1)^2-4P}}{2P}.
$$
The degradation-limited <diffusion> distances are proportional to $\ell_u=\sqrt{D_u/\beta_u}$ and $\ell_v=\sqrt{D_v/\beta_v}$. Thus $P=(\ell_v/\ell_u)^2$ and the criterion says **the <inhibitor> must travel more than $(1+\sqrt2)$ times as far as the <activator> before degradation**. Short-range activation and longer-range inhibition permit a local peak to reinforce itself. On a finite domain the allowed wavenumbers must also intersect the unstable band; the displayed condition assumes continuously available modes.

= e
{parent=13e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

At the threshold $P_*=3+2\sqrt2$, the <determinant> quadratic has a double zero at
$$
k_*^2=\frac{P_*-1}{2P_*}=P_*^{-1/2}=\sqrt2-1.
$$
Consequently the scaled disturbance length in $\cos(s/L)$ is
$$
\boxed{L_{\rm crit}=P_*^{1/4}=\sqrt{1+\sqrt2}.}
$$
The physical length is $\ell_uL_{\rm crit}=\sqrt{\ell_u\ell_v}$ at threshold; its full spatial <wavelength> is $2\pi$ times this length, not this length itself. For much shorter lengths, the large negative <diffusion> terms smooth both components. For much longer lengths, <diffusion> contributes little and the stable homogeneous reaction feedback, especially the <inhibitor> response, suppresses disturbances. The selected length lies between those two stabilization mechanisms.

= 14B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=14B}

Let the three orbit points be $a<b<c$. Up to reflection of the interval, their images are $F(a)=b,F(b)=c,F(c)=a$. Set $I=[a,b]$ and $J=[b,c]$. <Continuity> and the <intermediate value theorem> imply the covering relations
$$
F(I)\supseteq J,\qquad F(J)\supseteq I\cup J.
$$
We first prove the needed interval-covering principle using the permitted pullback fact. If a cyclic list of closed intervals $I_0,\ldots,I_{n-1},I_n=I_0$ obeys $F(I_j)\supseteq I_{j+1}$, pull back successively from the end to obtain a closed interval $K\subseteq I_0$ with $F^n(K)=I_0$ and $F^j(K)\subseteq I_j$. Since $F^n(K)\supseteq K$, the <intermediate value theorem> gives a <fixed point> of $F^n$ in $K$: choose preimages of the two endpoints of $K$, which give opposite weak signs of $F^n(x)-x$, irrespective of their order. This point realizes the cyclic itinerary.

For $n\geq2$, use the word $I J^{n-1}$. It obeys the covering relations and therefore yields a point with period dividing $n$. A point away from the shared endpoint $b$ has an unambiguous itinerary, and this word has exactly one $I$ per cycle; it cannot be a repetition of a shorter word. Its least period is consequently $n$. If an orbit touches $b$, it is the original three-cycle. That realizes the case $n=3$ itself. It cannot realize any of the other words: for $n$ not divisible by three it is not fixed by $F^n$, and for a multiple of three larger than three it visits $a\in I\setminus J$ more than once, contradicting the itinerary. Thus the construction works for every $n\geq2$. For $n=1$, $F(J)\supseteq J$ and the same fixed-point argument gives a <fixed point>. This proves <period three implies all periods>: **there is an orbit of every positive least period**.

For the <two five-cycles forced by a three-cycle>, use the two cyclic words $I J J J J$ and $I J I J J$. Both are admissible. Their points cannot be endpoints from the three-cycle, and because five is prime and the words use both intervals, each has least period five. Their itineraries have respectively one and two visits to the interior of $I$; cyclically shifting the starting point cannot change this count. Hence they give **at least two distinct period-five orbits**.

= 15D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= a
{parent=15d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the Newtonian dust calculation, use a comoving spherical shell of physical radius $R(t)=a(t)\chi$. Homogeneity gives its enclosed mass $M=4\pi\rho R^3/3$, and the shell theorem removes the gravitational force of exterior shells. Assume pressureless matter, conserved enclosed mass, no <cosmological constant>, and no shell crossing. Its specific conserved mechanical <energy> is
$$
\mathcal E=\frac12\dot R^2-\frac{GM}{R}
=\chi^2\left(\frac12\dot a^2-\frac{4\pi G}{3}\rho a^2\right).
$$
Homogeneity permits the <energy> per $\chi^2$ to be a constant, written $-kc^2/2$. Dividing by $a^2$ gives
$$
\boxed{H^2+\frac{kc^2}{a^2}=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho,\qquad H=\dot a/a.}
$$
This is a Newtonian derivation for dust. For radiation the enclosed gravitating mass is not conserved, so this same mechanical-energy argument cannot simply be reused; general relativity supplies the same Friedmann constraint with the total <energy density> divided by $c^2$, together with pressure-dependent fluid conservation.

= b
{parent=15d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The fluid conservation equation is $\dot\rho+3H(\rho+P/c^2)=0$. With the radiation equation of state, it becomes $\dot\rho+4H\rho=0$, giving $\rho a^4=\rho_0$. Define the <critical density> $\rho_{\rm crit}=3H^2/(8\pi G)$ and <cosmological density parameter> $\Omega=\rho/\rho_{\rm crit}$. Since $a_0=1$,
$$
\boxed{\rho=\frac{3H_0^2\Omega_0}{8\pi G a^4}.}
$$
Evaluating the <Friedmann equation> today gives $H_0^2+kc^2=H_0^2\Omega_0$, hence
$$
\boxed{kc^2=H_0^2(\Omega_0-1),\qquad
H^2=H_0^2\Omega_0\left(a^{-4}-\beta a^{-2}\right),
\quad\beta=\frac{\Omega_0-1}{\Omega_0}.}
$$

= c
{parent=15d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $\Omega_0>1$, introduce <conformal time> by $dt/d\tau=a$. Since $a'=a\dot a$, the constraint becomes
$$
(a')^2=H_0^2\Omega_0(1-\beta a^2).
$$
On the initial expanding branch, separate variables and choose $a=t=\tau=0$ at the <big bang>. With $\alpha=H_0\sqrt{\Omega_0-1}$,
$$
\boxed{a(\tau)=\frac{\sin(\alpha\tau)}{\sqrt\beta},\qquad
 t(\tau)=\frac{1-\cos(\alpha\tau)}{\alpha\sqrt\beta}.}
$$
The second formula follows by integrating $dt/d\tau=a$. The model reaches maximum size at $\alpha\tau=\pi/2$ and recollapses at $\alpha\tau=\pi$.

Today $a=1$ and $H_0>0$ selects the expanding branch, so
$$
\tau_0=\alpha^{-1}\arcsin\sqrt\beta,
\qquad\cos(\alpha\tau_0)=\sqrt{1-\beta}=\Omega_0^{-1/2}.
$$
Substitution yields the requested age
$$
\boxed{t_0=H_0^{-1}\frac{\sqrt{\Omega_0}-1}{\Omega_0-1}
=\frac1{H_0(\sqrt{\Omega_0}+1)}.}
$$
In particular the numerator is $\sqrt{\Omega_0}-1$, not $\sqrt{\Omega_0-1}$; the PDF displays the former. The flat-model limit is $1/(2H_0)$.

= 16F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=16F}

The inductive definition of <ordinal addition> is
$$
\alpha+0=\alpha,\qquad\alpha+(\beta+1)=(\alpha+\beta)+1,
\qquad\alpha+\lambda=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}(\alpha+\beta)
$$
for nonzero limit <ordinals> $\lambda$. The synthetic definition is the <order type> of a disjoint copy of $\alpha$ followed by a disjoint copy of $\beta$, retaining the order within each block and declaring every element of the first block smaller than every element of the second.

These are equivalent by <transfinite induction> in the second argument. An empty second block leaves $\alpha$; a successor block appends one final point; and a limit block is the increasing union of its proper initial blocks, whose order types have the indicated <supremum>. Thus the synthetic construction satisfies exactly the inductive recursion, which determines the operation uniquely. It is not commutative: **$1+\omega=\omega$, whereas $\omega+1>\omega$**.

= i
{parent=16f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

This assertion is **always true**. Synthetically, either bracketing places three disjoint blocks of order types $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ in that order. The identity on these blocks is an <order isomorphism>, so both sums have the same <order type>. This proves <associativity of ordinal addition> for successor and limit <ordinals> alike.

= ii
{parent=16f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

This assertion is **false**, even for nonzero limit <ordinals>. Take $\alpha=\omega$ and $\beta=\omega^2$. Then $\omega+\omega^2=\omega^2$, while $\omega^2+\omega>\omega^2$. The first identity follows from the limit recursion, since $\sup_{n<\omega}(\omega+\omega n)=\omega^2$; the second sum strictly extends the initial block of type $\omega^2$.

= iii
{parent=16f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

This assertion is **false**. Set $\alpha=1$ and $\beta=\omega_1$. The limit recursion gives
$$
1+\omega_1=\sup_{\gamma<\omega_1}(1+\gamma)=\omega_1.
$$
Every term is <countable>, and they are cofinal in the <first uncountable ordinal>. Yet $\alpha=1$ is neither zero nor $\omega_1$.

= iv
{parent=16f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

This assertion is **always true**. <Ordinal addition> obeys $\alpha+\beta\geq\alpha$ and $\alpha+\beta\geq\beta$. Thus both summands are at most $\omega_1$. If $0<\beta<\omega_1$ and $\alpha<\omega_1$, both <ordinals> are <countable>, and their disjoint ordered union is <countable>, so their sum cannot be $\omega_1$. If instead $\alpha=\omega_1$ and $\beta>0$, appending a nonempty second block gives a sum strictly larger than $\omega_1$. These exhaust the possibilities with $0<\beta<\omega_1$; hence **$\beta=0$ or $\beta=\omega_1$**.

= 17F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=17F}

First suppose $G$ is 3-connected and noncomplete, and write $\Delta=\Delta(G)$. There is an induced path $a,v,b$ with $a,b$ nonadjacent: otherwise the first three vertices of any shortest path between nonadjacent vertices would contradict that assertion. Since $G$ is 3-connected, $G-\{a,b\}$ is a <connected graph>. Choose a <spanning tree> of that graph rooted at $v$. Color $a,b$ first with the same color, then color the other vertices in reverse tree order, leaving $v$ last. Every vertex except $v$ has its tree parent uncolored and thus at most $\Delta-1$ previously colored neighbors. At $v$, the two neighbors $a,b$ share a color, so again at most $\Delta-1$ colors are forbidden. This constructs a proper $\Delta$-coloring and proves **Brooks' bound for 3-connected noncomplete graphs**. A <complete graph> is exactly the excluded case; a <3-connected graph> cannot be an <odd cycle>.

For the partition claim, assume $d_1,d_2$ are nonnegative integers with $d_1+d_2=\Delta-1$. Minimize
$$
\Phi=d_2e(G[V_1])+d_1e(G[V_2]).
$$
If a vertex in $V_1$ has internal degree $a\geq d_1+1$, its degree $b$ into $V_2$ is at most $d_2$. Moving it to $V_2$ changes $\Phi$ by $d_1b-d_2a\leq-d_2$, contradicting minimality when $d_2>0$. The analogous move handles a violation in $V_2$ when $d_1>0$. For a zero coefficient, break ties among minimizers by minimizing the total number of internal edges. If $d_2=0<d_1$, minimality of $\Phi=d_1e(G[V_2])$ ensures $V_2$ is <independent>; a violating vertex in $V_1$ has all its $\Delta=d_1+1$ neighbors in $V_1$ and no neighbor in $V_2$, so moving it reduces the tie-break quantity without increasing $\Phi$. The reversed argument handles $d_1=0$. If both are zero then $\Delta=1$, and the graph is a union of isolated vertices and edges, admitting two <independent> parts. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Delta(G[V_i])\leq d_i\quad(i=1,2).}
$$
For $\Delta\geq5$, put $d_1=3,d_2=\Delta-4$. A maximum-degree-three graph containing no $K_4$ is 3-colorable. Here is why the previously proved 3-connected case suffices: <connected> graphs of degree at most two are paths or cycles and use at most three colors. Split a larger graph at cut vertices and permute colors when gluing. In a 2-connected cubic graph with a two-vertex cut $\{u,v\}$, a cut edge $uv$ gives colorings of the sides glued with their distinct endpoint colors. If $u,v$ are nonadjacent, the cut components have positive numbers of edges to both endpoints and the total endpoint degrees are at most three. There are at most three components. With three components, adding $uv$ to each side preserves <maximum degree> three and gives the endpoints degree two. No new $K_4$ is created, so induction supplies side colorings with distinct endpoint colors, which can be permuted to agree. With two components, the components incident twice to $u$ and twice to $v$ either agree or differ. If they differ, adding $uv$ to each side preserves <maximum degree> three; each augmented side is smaller and cannot be $K_4$, because one endpoint then has degree only two, so induction gives distinct-endpoint colorings. If they agree, call the side with only one edge from each endpoint the small side. Any coloring of the other side can be matched on it: for equal endpoint colors, contract $u,v$ in the small side (the merged vertex has degree at most two); for distinct colors add $uv$ (the endpoints again have degree two). Induction applies in both cases. A 2-connected graph with no two-vertex cut is 3-connected and was handled above. This proves the needed subcubic coloring fact without quoting the unproved full theorem.

Use three colors on $V_1$ and at most $d_2+1=\Delta-3$ fresh colors on $V_2$, by greedy coloring. This gives $\chi(G)\leq\Delta$. More usefully, repeatedly remove degree-at-most-three parts: the partition supplies a four-degree reduction for a cost of three colors. If $f(\Delta)=\frac34\Delta+\frac32$, then $3+f(\Delta-4)=f(\Delta)$. Induction in $\Delta$, with the remaining part still $K_4$-free, gives
$$
\boxed{\chi(G)\leq\frac34\Delta(G)+\frac32.}
$$
The base cases $\Delta=0,1,2,3$ use respectively $1,2,3,3$ colors, within this bound; at $\Delta=4$, Brooks' theorem would give four, but the same partition with $(3,0)$ gives a 3-colorable part and an <independent> part, hence four. Thus the induction requires no unproved general Brooks theorem.

= 18G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= i
{parent=18g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $a=0$, the <splitting field> is $K$ and its <Galois group> is trivial. Otherwise choose a root $\alpha$ and a primitive $n$th root of unity $\zeta\in K$. Every root is $\zeta^j\alpha$, so $L=K(\alpha)$. Each automorphism is determined by $\sigma(\alpha)/\alpha\in\mu_n$. This is an injective group homomorphism, since the <roots of unity> are fixed by every automorphism. A subgroup of the <cyclic group> $\mu_n$ is cyclic. Thus **$\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$ is cyclic**, with order dividing $n$.

= ii
{parent=18g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the indicated <cyclic Galois resolvent>, reindexing its terms and using $g^m=1$ gives
$$
gR(w)=\zeta R(w).
$$
There exists $w$ with $R(w)\ne0$ because distinct field <automorphisms> are <linearly independent> as <functions>. A short proof is as follows. Take a nonzero relation with the fewest nonzero terms, normalize one coefficient, evaluate it at $uv$ and subtract a scalar multiple of the relation at $v$. Choose $u$ on which two participating <automorphisms> differ. This produces a nonzero relation with fewer terms, a contradiction.

Set $\alpha=R(w)\ne0$. Then $g^j\alpha=\zeta^j\alpha$ are $m$ distinct conjugates. Also $g(\alpha^m)=\alpha^m$, so $a=\alpha^m\in K$. The orbit has size $m$, giving $[K(\alpha):K]=m$ and hence $K(\alpha)=M$. All the roots $\zeta^j\alpha$ belong to $M$, so
$$
\boxed{M\text{ is the splitting field of }X^m-a\text{ for }a=R(w)^m\in K.}
$$
This also treats $m=1$ with a nonzero $w\in K$.

= 19G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= i
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The irreducible <characters> are an <orthonormal basis> of the space of complex class <functions> for the <inner product>
$$
\langle f,h\rangle=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\overline{f(g)}h(g).
$$
Thus the row <orthogonality> relation and <completeness> statement are
$$
\boxed{\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\overline{\chi_i(g)}\chi_k(g)=\delta_{ik},
\qquad f=\sum_i\langle\chi_i,f\rangle\chi_i.}
$$
In particular the number of irreducible <characters> equals the number of conjugacy classes. In class-representative notation, the <orthogonality> relation is $\sum_j\overline{\chi_i(g_j)}\chi_k(g_j)/|Z(g_j)|=\delta_{ik}$.

= ii
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply <completeness> to the indicator $f_j$ of the <conjugacy class> of $g_j$. Its expansion coefficient is $\langle\chi_i,f_j\rangle=\overline{\chi_i(g_j)}/|Z(g_j)|$, because that class has size $|G|/|Z(g_j)|$. Evaluating at $g_k$ gives
$$
\delta_{jk}=\sum_i\frac{\overline{\chi_i(g_j)}\chi_i(g_k)}{|Z(g_j)|},
\qquad\boxed{\sum_i\overline{\chi_i(g_j)}\chi_i(g_k)=\delta_{jk}|Z(g_j)|.}
$$
The complex conjugation is part of the printed formula; dropping it would not give the general column <orthogonality> relation.

= iii
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <matrix> $A$ is square by <completeness>. Column <orthogonality> says $A^*A=\operatorname{diag}(|Z(g_j)|)$. Taking determinants gives
$$
\boxed{|\det A|^2=\det(A^*A)=\prod_j|Z(g_j)|.}
$$
In particular its <determinant> is nonzero.

= iv
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Complex conjugation permutes the irreducible <characters>, because the conjugate of an <irreducible representation> is irreducible. This involution fixes the real-valued <characters> and interchanges the other <characters> in pairs. If there are $r$ such pairs, then conjugating $A$ permutes its rows by $r$ transpositions. Therefore
$$
\overline{\det A}=(-1)^r\det A.
$$
It follows that **the <determinant> is real when $r$ is even and purely imaginary when $r$ is odd**. It never vanishes, by the preceding part. Real-valued <characters> include both real and <quaternionic representation> types; the criterion concerns values of the <characters>, not those two representation types separately.

= 20G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= Solution
{parent=20G}

The <ring of integers> is $\mathcal O_k=\mathbb Z[\sqrt7]$, since $7\equiv3\pmod4$, and its <discriminant> is $28$. Rationalizing gives
$$
\varepsilon=8+3\sqrt7,\qquad\varepsilon^{-1}=8-3\sqrt7,\qquad N(\varepsilon)=1.
$$
Both are algebraic integers, so **$\varepsilon$ is a <unit>**. Set $\mathfrak p=(3+\sqrt7)$. Since $(3+\sqrt7)^2=2\varepsilon$,
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak p^2=(2).}
$$
Also $N(\mathfrak p)=|N(3+\sqrt7)|=2$. It is the unique ideal of <ideal norm> two: any quotient of size two is $\mathbb F_2$, and the image of $\sqrt7$ must satisfy $z^2=1$, hence be one. The <kernel of a ring homomorphism> onto $\mathbb F_2$ is uniquely $(2,1+\sqrt7)$, which is $\mathfrak p$.

The given Minkowski constant means every <ideal class> has an integral representative of <ideal norm> at most $\frac12\sqrt{28}=\sqrt7<3$. Such a <ideal norm> is one or two. The norm-one ideal is the whole ring, and the sole norm-two ideal is principal. Thus **the <class number> is one**.

If $x^2-7y^2=2$, the <principal ideal> $(x+y\sqrt7)$ has <ideal norm> two and hence equals $\mathfrak p$. Its generator differs from $3+\sqrt7$ by a <unit>. Conversely multiplication by a norm-one <unit> preserves the <field norm> two. With the assumed <fundamental unit>, all <units> are $\pm\varepsilon^n$; in fact a norm-minus-one <unit> is impossible since $x^2\equiv-1\pmod7$ has no solution. Therefore the complete answer is
$$
\boxed{x+y\sqrt7=\pm(8+3\sqrt7)^n(3+\sqrt7),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
For $n=1$ and the positive sign, the product is $45+17\sqrt7$, giving **$(x,y)=(45,17)$**; $45^2-7\cdot17^2=2$ checks the result.

= 21H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= Solution
{parent=21H}

The <Seifert-van Kampen theorem> states that if a space is covered by path-connected open sets $U,V$ with path-connected intersection containing a basepoint, then its <fundamental group> is the <free product> $\pi_1(U)*\pi_1(V)$ modulo the <normal subgroup> generated by identifying the two images of each loop in $U\cap V$. More generally it is the corresponding pushout of groups.

A wedge of two circles has <fundamental group> $F(a,b)$, the <free group> on their two loops: apply the theorem to thickened circle neighborhoods whose intersection is <contractible> and use $\pi_1(S^1)=\mathbb Z$. Represent the <torus> by a square with opposite edges identified. Remove an interior disk; the remainder deformation retracts onto this wedge. Replacing the disk attaches a two-cell whose boundary follows $aba^{-1}b^{-1}$. A disk neighborhood and the punctured <torus> have annular intersection, and van Kampen kills precisely that boundary loop. Hence
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(T)=\langle a,b\mid aba^{-1}b^{-1}=1\rangle\cong\mathbb Z^2.}
$$
A <torus> with a point removed retracts to a <torus> with a small open disk removed, and then to the same wedge. Thus
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(T\setminus\{p\})\cong F_2.}
$$
For the <connected sum>, remove a disk from each <torus> and glue their boundary circles with reversed orientations. The two pieces have free groups $F(a,b)$ and $F(c,d)$ and their overlap is an annulus. Its boundary generators correspond to $[a,b]$ and $[c,d]^{-1}$, where $[a,b]=aba^{-1}b^{-1}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(T\#T)=\langle a,b,c,d\mid[a,b][c,d]=1\rangle.}
$$
The boundary relation is essential: this group is neither the <free group> on four generators nor the abelian group $\mathbb Z^4$.

= 22F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=22F}

A linear operator between Banach spaces is a <compact operator> when it maps the <unit ball> into a relatively <compact set>, equivalently when every bounded sequence of inputs has an image subsequence converging in <norm>. Such an operator is bounded. For a <bounded linear operator> on a complex <Banach space>, the <resolvent set> consists of the complex $\lambda$ for which $T-\lambda I$ is bijective with bounded inverse; its complement is the <spectrum>. The <point spectrum> consists of those $\lambda$ for which $T-\lambda I$ has a nonzero <kernel>, namely the <eigenvalues>.

On a <Hilbert space>, $T$ is <self-adjoint> if $T=T^*$, or equivalently $\langle Tx,y\rangle=\langle x,Ty\rangle$ for all vectors. The given prescription extends uniquely to
$$
Tx=\sum_{j\geq1}\frac{x_j}{j}e_j,\qquad x=\sum_jx_je_j.
$$
It is bounded with <norm> one. The real diagonal entries prove self-adjointness by the inner-product identity. Define the finite-rank truncation $T_mx=\sum_{j\leq m}x_je_j/j$. Then $\|T-T_m\|=1/(m+1)\to0$. To see compactness directly, extract successively convergent subsequences of the finitely many first coordinates from a bounded input sequence; the uniform tail bound just displayed makes the resulting image subsequence Cauchy. Hence **$T$ is compact and <self-adjoint>**.

Each $1/j$ is an <eigenvalue> with <eigenspace> $\mathbb Ce_j$. If $Tx=\lambda x$, each coordinate satisfies $(1/j-\lambda)x_j=0$, so there are no other <eigenvalues>; in particular zero is not an <eigenvalue>. But $\|Te_j\|=1/j\to0$, which contradicts a bounded inverse at zero. Thus zero is in the <spectrum>.

For $\lambda\notin\{0,1,1/2,1/3,\ldots\}$, its distance from this <closed set> is positive. The diagonal formula
$$
(T-\lambda I)^{-1}y=\sum_{j\geq1}\frac{y_j}{1/j-\lambda}e_j
$$
then defines a bounded inverse. This proves the complete description
$$
\boxed{\sigma(T)=\{0\}\cup\{1/j:j\geq1\},\quad
\sigma_p(T)=\{1/j:j\geq1\},\quad
\rho(T)=\mathbb C\setminus\sigma(T).}
$$

= 23H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=23H}

A <function element> is a pair $(f,D)$ where $D$ is a nonempty disk and $f$ is holomorphic there; the germ at its center captures its local information. Elements continue one another when their <functions> agree on an overlapping <connected> neighborhood. A <complete analytic function> is a maximal collection reachable from one element by chains of <analytic continuation>, including all further continuations of its members.

If $f^n=p$ in the initial disk, the <identity theorem> propagates this identity across every overlapping disk in a continuation chain. Induction along the chain proves **every element of the <complete analytic function> also satisfies $\widetilde f^{\,n}=p$**.

Put $F(z,w)=w^n-p(z)$. Its gradient is $(-p'(z),nw^{n-1})$. At a point of the curve with $w\ne0$, the second entry is nonzero; at $w=0$, the root of $p$ is simple and the first entry is nonzero. Thus the <holomorphic implicit function theorem> supplies charts. Its precise local statement is that a holomorphic equation with nonzero derivative in one variable can locally be solved uniquely and holomorphically for that variable as a function of the other. For $w\ne0$, use $z$ as coordinate and write $w=w(z)$; at a simple root use $w$ as coordinate and write $z=z(w)$. The transition maps are holomorphic with nonzero derivatives, giving a <Riemann surface>. The two <projections> are holomorphic because they are holomorphic expressions in either kind of chart.

Away from the zeros of $p$, the projection onto $z$ is an unramified $n$-sheeted covering. A germ of a root corresponds to $(z,f(z))$ and its analytic continuations follow this covering. Near a simple root, $z-z_0$ is a nonzero analytic factor times $w^n$; a circuit about $z_0$ cyclically permutes all $n$ branches. For nonconstant square-free $p$, these circuits connect all sheets. Hence the germ surface $S(F)$ is the curve with the points over the roots removed, and **$C$ is its branched completion obtained by adding those ramification points**. An ordinary function element in the variable $z$ cannot include such a point: the zero order of a holomorphic $n$th power is divisible by $n$, whereas $p$ has zero order one. If a convention already includes these branch points in the <Riemann surface> of a multivalued function, that completed surface is $C$ itself. For a nonzero constant $p$, the curve has $n$ separate components, and the chosen element determines just one of them. The identically zero polynomial is excluded by the non-singularity premise.

= 24H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=24H}

The plane <planar isoperimetric inequality> says that a simple closed curve of length $L$ enclosing area $A$ satisfies $L^2\geq4\pi A$, with equality for circles.

For sufficiently small $r$, below the <injectivity radius> at $p$, <geodesic polar coordinates> give the surface metric $dr^2+j(r,\theta)^2d\theta^2$. The radial variation field is a <Jacobi field>, so
$$
j_{rr}+K(\gamma_\theta(r))j=0,\qquad j(0,\theta)=0,\quad j_r(0,\theta)=1.
$$
Its integral equation is $j=r-\int_0^r(r-u)K(\gamma_\theta(u))j(u,\theta)\,du$. <Continuity> of <curvature> and $j(u,\theta)=u+o(u)$, uniformly in direction, give
$$
j(r,\theta)=r-\frac{K(p)}6r^3+o(r^3).
$$
The <Gauss lemma> makes the angular metric factor both the circle line element and the polar area factor. Therefore
$$
L=\int_0^{2\pi}j(r,\theta)\,d\theta
=2\pi r-\frac\pi3K(p)r^3+o(r^3),
$$
$$
A=\int_0^r\int_0^{2\pi}j(u,\theta)\,d\theta\,du
=\pi r^2-\frac\pi{12}K(p)r^4+o(r^4).
$$
Squaring the length expansion and subtracting gives
$$
\boxed{4\pi A-L^2=\pi^2K(p)r^4+o(r^4).}
$$
This identifies the requested remainder and proves its ratio to $r^4$ tends to zero. For $K(p)>0$, sufficiently small <geodesic> disks have **$L^2<4\pi A$**, the opposite strict inequality to the planar bound. Their positive <curvature> allows more area for the same perimeter; the Euclidean inequality is not a universal inequality for curved surfaces.

= 25J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= Solution
{parent=25J}

A family $\mathcal R$ is <uniformly integrable> if
$$
\lim_{K\to\infty}\sup_{X\in\mathcal R}E[|X|\mathbf1_{\{|X|>K\}}]=0.
$$
This implies integrability and a uniform bound on $E|X|$, since $E|X|\leq K+E[|X|\mathbf1_{\{|X|>K\}}]$. <Convergence in probability> means $P(|X_n-X|>\varepsilon)\to0$ for every $\varepsilon>0$; convergence in $L^1$ means $E|X_n-X|\to0$. The latter implies the former by <Markov's inequality>. Conversely, <convergence in probability> together with <uniform integrability> of $\{X_n\}$ implies that $X$ is integrable and $X_n\to X$ in $L^1$. Moreover an $L^1$-convergent sequence is <uniformly integrable>. This is the criterion that excludes rare very large values from carrying a nonvanishing first-moment error.

For the sum family, use the pointwise bound
$$
|X+Y|\mathbf1_{\{|X+Y|>K\}}
\leq2|X|\mathbf1_{\{|X|>K/2\}}+2|Y|\mathbf1_{\{|Y|>K/2\}}.
$$
Indeed, if the left indicator is one then the larger of $|X|,|Y|$ exceeds $K/2$, and twice that larger number bounds $|X+Y|$. Taking <expectations> and the <supremum> over $\mathcal R_1,\mathcal R_2$ bounds the sum-family tail by the sum of their two uniformly vanishing tails. Thus **the whole family of sums is <uniformly integrable>**, with no <independence> assumption.

= 26I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= Solution
{parent=26I}

A <renewal process> counts arrivals $N(t)=\max\{j:A_j\leq t\}$, where $A_0=0,A_j=S_1+\cdots+S_j$ and the holding times are <independent>, identically distributed positive random variables. Their sum tends to infinity almost surely, so the count is finite on bounded time intervals. Use $N(s)=X_s$ in the question's notation. The index $J=N(s)+1=\min\{j:A_j>s\}$ is a stopping index for the holding-time sequence. Conditional on $J=j$ and the first $j$ holding times, every later $S_{j+k}$ retains its original <probability distribution>. Summing over $j$ proves
$$
\boxed{P(T_n>t)=P(S_1>t)\quad(n\geq2).}
$$
The straddling time $T_1=S_J$ is different. Let $\overline F(t)=P(S_1>t)$ and let $U$ be the <renewal measure> $U(B)=\sum_{j\geq0}P(A_j\in B)$. <Independence> of the next <holding time> from its start gives
$$
P(T_1>t)=\int_{[0,s]}\overline F(\max(t,s-u))\,U(du),
\qquad1=\int_{[0,s]}\overline F(s-u)\,U(du).
$$
For two numbers in $[0,1]$, their minimum is at least their product. Since $\overline F(\max(t,a))=\min(\overline F(t),\overline F(a))$, subtracting $\overline F(t)$ times the second integral proves
$$
\boxed{P(T_1>t)\geq P(S_1>t).}
$$
These formulae handle holding-time atoms with the stated renewal convention $A_j\leq s<A_{j+1}$; sums may be interchanged by nonnegativity.

For rate-$\lambda$ exponentials, the <renewal process> is Poisson. The elapsed age $B_s=s-A_{N(s)}$ has <probability distribution> $\min(E,s)$ with $E\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$: for $0\leq b<s$, $P(B_s>b)$ is the <probability> of no arrivals in $(s-b,s]$, namely $e^{-\lambda b}$, with an atom $e^{-\lambda s}$ at $s$. The forward residual $R_s$ is an <independent> rate-$\lambda$ exponential by <independent> future increments. Thus $T_1=B_s+R_s$. Conditioning on the age gives the exact survival law
$$
\boxed{P(T_1>t)=e^{-\lambda t}\big[1+\lambda\min(s,t)\big],\qquad t\geq0.}
$$
For $s,t>0$ the extra term is strictly positive, proving strict inequality. For fixed $t$ and $s\to\infty$, the limit is $e^{-\lambda t}(1+\lambda t)$, which equals the survival <probability> of the sum of two <independent> rate-$\lambda$ exponentials. This is the finite-time form of the <stochastic length bias of a renewal interval>.

= 27I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= i
{parent=27i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $Q=P_1-P_0$ and $R=I-P_1$. For nested subspaces these are orthogonal <projections> onto $\Theta_1\cap\Theta_0^\perp$ and $\Theta_1^\perp$, of ranks $d_1-d_0$ and $d-d_1$. Under the null, both annihilate $\mu$. In an <orthonormal basis> adapted to $\Theta_0$, these two spaces and their complement, the coordinates of $X-\mu$ are <independent> $N(0,\sigma^2)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{\|X-P_1X\|^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi^2_{d-d_1},\qquad
\frac{\|P_1X-P_0X\|^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi^2_{d_1-d_0},}
$$
and the two variables are <independent>. Equivalently the <joint probability density> of the two unscaled sums $a,b>0$ factors into gamma <densities> of respective shapes $(d-d_1)/2,(d_1-d_0)/2$ and common scale $2\sigma^2$.

The nuisance <variance> cancels in
$$
\boxed{F=\frac{\|P_1X-P_0X\|^2/(d_1-d_0)}{\|X-P_1X\|^2/(d-d_1)}
\sim F_{d_1-d_0,d-d_1}\quad\text{under }H_0.}
$$
Reject for a value above the upper $\alpha$ critical quantile to obtain a level-$\alpha$ test. Under a genuine alternative the numerator is noncentral chi-square with noncentrality $\|Q\mu\|^2/\sigma^2$, while the denominator retains its central law, explaining the upper-tail choice.

= ii
{parent=27i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $I,J\geq2$. The additive two-way model has dimension $1+(I-1)+(J-1)=I+J-1$; the null model, with no exam effects, has dimension $I$. Write row, column and grand means as $\overline X_{i\cdot},\overline X_{\cdot j},\overline X_{\cdot\cdot}$. <Orthogonal projection> onto the full model gives fitted values $\overline X_{i\cdot}+\overline X_{\cdot j}-\overline X_{\cdot\cdot}$, while the null fit is $\overline X_{i\cdot}$. The added exam sum of squares and full residual sum of squares are
$$
SS_E=I\sum_{j=1}^J(\overline X_{\cdot j}-\overline X_{\cdot\cdot})^2,
$$
$$
SS_R=\sum_{i,j}(X_{ij}-\overline X_{i\cdot}-\overline X_{\cdot j}+\overline X_{\cdot\cdot})^2.
$$
The preceding orthogonal-projection result gives <independent> null chi-squares with $J-1$ and $(I-1)(J-1)$ <degrees of freedom>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F=\frac{SS_E/(J-1)}{SS_R/[(I-1)(J-1)]}\sim F_{J-1,(I-1)(J-1)}.}
$$
Reject the no-exam-effect hypothesis for a large value. The printed alternative repeats the identifying constraint $\sum_j\beta_j=0$; a genuine alternative additionally has at least one nonzero $\beta_j$. No interaction term is being fitted; the <independent> equal-variance Gaussian error assumption is what makes this exact test valid.

= 28J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= i
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $\mathcal F_n$ represent the information at decision time $n$ and $E_n$ <conditional expectation> given it. $V_n(w)$ is the largest conditional expected utility from the remaining <consumption> opportunities with current wealth $w$; it may depend on the information state as well as $w$. The controls $c$ and $\theta$ are chosen using current information. In the usual allocation interpretation $0\leq c\leq w$ and $0\leq\theta\leq1$, and next wealth is $(w-c)[r+\theta(X_{n+1}-r)]$. Other borrowing/short-selling rules require explicitly replacing this admissible set while preserving solvency.

The <Bellman equation> follows by separating current utility from future utility: after any current decision the best continuation has conditional value $V_{n+1}$, and maximizing over the current admissible decisions gives the stated recursion. At the last opportunity all wealth is consumed, because utility is increasing, giving $V_N(w)=U(w)$.

There are two printed qualifications. Strict convexity is not needed for this recursion, but it conflicts with the concave power utility in part (ii) and does not support the interior maximizing first-order conditions in part (iii). Also the displayed recursion starting at $n=0$ includes <consumption> at times $0,\ldots,N$, whereas the preceding sum starts at one. The same recursion represents that sum when begun at time one; alternatively relabel the <consumption> dates. Below the policy is given for the recursion's remaining horizon, so this indexing discrepancy does not leave the optimization unspecified.

= ii
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $U'(x)=x^{-R}>0$ and $U''(x)=-Rx^{-R-1}<0$: the specified utility is <strictly concave>, contrary to the word “convex” in part (i). Assume its expected values are finite on the admissible set. Define $G_\theta=r+\theta(X-r)$ and choose a constant <portfolio> fraction $\theta_*$ maximizing $E[U(G_\theta)]$. Equivalently maximize $E[G_\theta^{1-R}]$ for $0<R<1$ and minimize it for $R>1$. An interior optimum satisfies
$$
\boxed{E[(X-r)G_{\theta_*}^{-R}]=0.}
$$
For a constrained endpoint optimum the corresponding derivative has the appropriate one-sided sign. The objective is concave in $\theta$ and <strictly concave> if $P(X\ne r)>0$, so this condition or its endpoint version determines the optimum. Put $\kappa=E[G_{\theta_*}^{1-R}]$ and $\gamma=\kappa^{1/R}>0$.

Backward induction with <independence> of the returns gives $V_n(w)=A_nw^{1-R}/(1-R)$ and $A_N=1$. Maximizing the <consumption> expression
$$
\frac{c^{1-R}+A_{n+1}\kappa(w-c)^{1-R}}{1-R}
$$
gives $c^{-R}=A_{n+1}\kappa(w-c)^{-R}$, hence
$$
c_n^*=\frac{w_n}{1+(A_{n+1}\kappa)^{1/R}},\qquad
A_n=\big[1+(A_{n+1}\kappa)^{1/R}\big]^R.
$$
Writing $b_n=A_n^{1/R}$ yields $b_N=1$ and $b_n=1+\gamma b_{n+1}$. Therefore the fully explicit horizon dependence is
$$
\boxed{b_n=\sum_{j=0}^{N-n}\gamma^j,\qquad
c_n^*=\frac{w_n}{b_n},\qquad\theta_n^*=\theta_*.}
$$
Use $b_n=(1-\gamma^{N-n+1})/(1-\gamma)$ when $\gamma\ne1$, and $b_n=N-n+1$ when $\gamma=1$. The investor consumes this fraction, invests the remaining wealth with the same optimal <portfolio> mix at each date, and consumes all remaining wealth at $N$. If the stock is identical to the bank almost surely, every <portfolio> fraction is equivalent.

= iii
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The equalities are valid at a differentiable interior optimum under the intended concave formulation, but not under the printed assumptions alone. To derive the intended result, differentiate the finite conditional sum in the Bellman objective. For an interior <portfolio> fraction with positive invested wealth, its derivative gives
$$
0=E_n[V_{n+1}'(w_{n+1}^*)(X_{n+1}-r)].
$$
The <consumption> derivative gives $U'(c_n^*)=E_n[V_{n+1}'(w_{n+1}^*)G_{\theta_n^*}]$. Since $G_\theta=r+\theta(X-r)$, the first equality cancels its risky part and yields the second requested equality. The envelope derivative with respect to wealth gives $V_n'(w_n^*)=E_n[V_{n+1}'(w_{n+1}^*)G_{\theta_n^*}]$, giving the third. Thus, under these added hypotheses,
$$
\boxed{E_n[V_{n+1}'(w_{n+1}^*)(X_{n+1}-r)]=0,
\qquad U'(c_n^*)=rE_n[V_{n+1}'(w_{n+1}^*)]=V_n'(w_n^*).}
$$
The first prices the excess stock payoff using future marginal utility; the second equates marginal utility from consuming now to that from saving in the bank; the third identifies the marginal value of wealth with that common quantity. At <portfolio> or <consumption> boundaries these are replaced by one-sided optimality inequalities. Finiteness of the sample space justifies differentiating a finite sum; it does not ensure differentiability of a <supremum> or an interior optimum.

A concrete counterexample satisfies the printed convex assumptions and attains every <supremum>. Take one remaining decision, a one-point sample space, a deterministic stock return $X=x>r>1$, $U(c)=c^2$, and controls in $[0,w]\times[0,1]$. The optimum is $c^*=0,\theta^*=1$, since the objective $c^2+x^2(w-c)^2$ is convex in $c$ and its larger endpoint value is $x^2w^2$. Then $w_{n+1}^*=xw$ and $V_{n+1}'=2xw$. The first claimed <expectation> is $2xw(x-r)>0$; the second compares $2rxw>0$ to $U'(0)=0$; the third compares $2rxw$ to $V_n'(w)=2x^2w$. All three fail. Consequently **the requested equalities cannot be proved as printed**; the derivation above supplies their precise intended interior version.

= 29I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= Solution
{parent=29I}

A time-homogeneous <Markov decision process> specifies a state space, admissible actions at each state, an action-dependent <transition kernel> and one-step reward, with neither <kernel> nor reward depending on time. A policy chooses actions using the observed history. The positive programming case has nonnegative rewards and maximizes expected undiscounted total reward, allowing infinite values; its finite-horizon values increase to the infinite-horizon <supremum> under the appropriate approximation argument.

Here let $\tau_B$ be the absorption time of the underlying <simple random walk> at $0,N$. Every admissible <stopping time> $T$ is at most $\tau_B$. The reward is bounded by $R_{\max}=\max_jr(j)$, and $E_i\tau_B=i(N-i)$, as follows by solving $h_i=1+(h_{i-1}+h_{i+1})/2$ with zero endpoint values. In particular absorption is almost surely finite. Truncating any policy at $T\wedge n$ changes its reward <expectation> by at most $2R_{\max}P_i(\tau_B>n)\leq2R_{\max}i(N-i)/n$. Since the truncated policy is allowed in the $n$-step problem,
$$
V_n(i)\leq V(i)\leq V_n(i)+2R_{\max}P_i(\tau_B>n).
$$
This proves **$V_n\to V$**, even uniformly on the finite state space.

The finite-horizon recursion starts with $V_0=r$, fixes the endpoint rewards and has
$$
V_{n+1}(i)=\max\{r(i),\tfrac12[V_n(i-1)+V_n(i+1)]\}.
$$
Taking limits shows $V\geq r$ and $2V(i)\geq V(i-1)+V(i+1)$, so $V$ is discretely concave. If $W$ is any <concave function> majorizing $r$, induction in this same recursion gives $V_n\leq W$, and hence $V\leq W$. Therefore **$V$ is the smallest <concave majorant> of the rewards**. At a point where $V(i)>r(i)$ the recursion forces equality with the neighboring average; the value graph is linear through each consecutive continuation region.

An optimal policy stops upon first entering the contact set $\{i:V(i)=r(i)\}$ and continues elsewhere. Before stopping the neighboring-average equality makes $V$ of the walk a bounded <martingale>; the <stopping time> is bounded by $\tau_B$. Applying the bounded stopping identity by first truncating the time and then passing to the limit proves the expected terminal reward equals $V(i)$.

Distinct rewards do not guarantee uniqueness. With $N=2$ and $r(0)=0,r(1)=1,r(2)=2$, the reward function is already linear and $V=r$. From state one, stopping gives one, while continuing to the next step also gives expected reward $(0+2)/2=1$. Both policies are optimal. Thus **the claimed uniqueness is false** even when all rewards are distinct.

= 30C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Partial Differential Equations}

= Solution
{parent=30C}

A <fundamental solution> of a linear <differential operator> $P$ is a <distribution> $G$ satisfying $PG=\delta_0$. The locally integrable function $G=e^{-|x|}/2$ defines a <distribution> by integration against compactly supported smooth test <functions>. Away from zero it satisfies $-G''+G=0$. It is continuous at zero, while $G'(0+)=-1/2$ and $G'(0-)=1/2$. Consequently its distributional second derivative is its ordinary derivative on the two sides plus the derivative jump times $\delta_0$:
$$
G''=G-\delta_0,\qquad\boxed{(-d^2/dx^2+1)G=\delta_0.}
$$
This derivative-jump formula follows by <integration by parts> on each half-line; the boundary terms equal $-\phi(0)$ in $G''$.

<Convolution> with the indicator forcing gives
$$
u_0(x)=\frac12\int_{-1}^1e^{-|x-y|}\,dy
=\boxed{\begin{cases}1-e^{-1}\cosh x,&|x|\leq1,\\
\sinh(1)e^{-|x|},&|x|\geq1.\end{cases}}
$$
The two values and their first derivatives agree at $\pm1$, so no delta terms arise there. This is a decaying <weak solution>, with the equation holding classically away from the forcing jumps.

For real Schwartz $\phi$, expand the <energy>:
$$
I[u_0+\phi]-I[u_0]
=\int(u_0'\phi'+u_0\phi-V\phi)\,dx
+\frac12\int[(\phi')^2+\phi^2]\,dx.
$$
The mixed term is zero by the weak equation $-u_0''+u_0=V$, or <integration by parts> using the continuous first derivative and decay. The remaining integral is strictly positive whenever $\phi$ is not identically zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{I[u_0+\phi]>I[u_0]\quad(0\ne\phi\text{ a real Schwartz function}).}
$$
The real-valued function space is implicit in the stated ordered quadratic functional; for complex <functions> one instead uses absolute squares and the corresponding real part of the forcing term.

= 31C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= a
{parent=31c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a localized traveling wave put $q(x,t)=Q(\xi)$ with $\xi=x-ct-x_0$, and require $Q,Q',Q''\to0$ at infinity. The modified KdV equation integrates once to $Q''-cQ+2Q^3=0$. Multiplying by $Q'$ and integrating again gives
$$
(Q')^2=cQ^2-Q^4.
$$
A nonzero decaying real solution requires $c>0$. Separating on the rising or falling half of the wave and using the indicated inverse-hyperbolic-secant integral yields
$$
\boxed{q(x,t)=\pm\sqrt c\,\operatorname{sech}\big[\sqrt c(x-ct-x_0)\big],\qquad c>0.}
$$
The signs and translations are arbitrary. Differentiating the sech profile verifies $Q''=cQ-2Q^3$ directly. The relevant antiderivative is the inverse hyperbolic secant, since $d(\operatorname{arcsech}x)/dx=-1/[x\sqrt{1-x^2}]$ for $0<x<1$.

= b
{parent=31c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The scaling of the equation suggests $q=t^{-1/3}v(z)$, $z=xt^{-1/3}$, for $t>0$. Substitution gives
$$
v^{(3)}+6v^2v'-\frac13(v+zv')=0.
$$
Integrating in $z$ gives the scaled <Painleve II equation>
$$
\boxed{v''=\frac z3v-2v^3+C,\qquad q(x,t)=t^{-1/3}v(xt^{-1/3}),}
$$
where $C$ is an arbitrary integration constant. If the standard normalization $w''=2w^3+sw+\alpha$ is desired, set $z=3^{1/3}s$ and $v(z)=i3^{-1/3}w(s)$. Then $\alpha=-3iC$ and
$$
q(x,t)=i(3t)^{-1/3}w\big(x/(3t)^{1/3}\big).
$$
The direct scaled equation avoids introducing imaginary variables when a real $v$ is required.

= c
{parent=31c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The given Riccati ansatz is $v'=-iv^2+iz/6$. Differentiating it and substituting for $v'$ shows
$$
v''=-2ivv'+i/6=-2v^3+zv/3+i/6.
$$
It therefore selects $C=i/6$ in the preceding scaled Painleve equation. Write $v=-i\Psi'/\Psi$. The quadratic terms cancel in the ansatz, leaving
$$
-i\left(\frac{\Psi''}{\Psi}+\frac z6\right)=0,
\qquad\Psi''+\frac z6\Psi=0.
$$
Thus every nonzero solution of the indicated <Airy equation> gives the local mKdV solution
$$
\boxed{q(x,t)=-it^{-1/3}\frac{\Psi'(xt^{-1/3})}{\Psi(xt^{-1/3})},\qquad t>0,}
$$
on intervals where $\Psi\ne0$. For example $\Psi(z)=A\operatorname{Ai}(-z/6^{1/3})+B\operatorname{Bi}(-z/6^{1/3})$, with constants not both zero, has the required equation. This branch is generally complex and meromorphic; the printed ansatz itself includes $i$. A real $v$ on a real interval would require simultaneously $v'=0$ and $v^2=z/6$, which is impossible on an interval. Hence this particular ansatz does not furnish a nontrivial real-valued mKdV profile.

= 32D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=32D}

Taking $n=e_i$ gives $\sigma_i^2=I$. Taking $n=(e_i+e_j)/\sqrt2$ for $i\ne j$ and expanding the square gives $\sigma_i\sigma_j+\sigma_j\sigma_i=0$. Thus the <anticommutator> and <commutator> together imply
$$
\sigma_i\sigma_j=\delta_{ij}I+i\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k,
\qquad\boxed{(a\cdot\sigma)(b\cdot\sigma)=(a\cdot b)I+i(a\times b)\cdot\sigma.}
$$
Since $n\cdot\sigma$ is <Hermitian> and $\theta$ is real, the exponent is <anti-Hermitian>. Its exponential has inverse equal to its adjoint and is therefore <unitary>. Splitting the <exponential series> into even and odd powers, using $(n\cdot\sigma)^2=I$, gives
$$
\boxed{U=I\cos(\theta/2)-i(n\cdot\sigma)\sin(\theta/2).}
$$
Put $N=n\cdot\sigma,M=m\cdot\sigma$ with $m\perp n$. Then $NM=i(n\times m)\cdot\sigma$, $MN=-NM$, and $NMN=-M$. Expanding $(c-iNs)M(c+iNs)$ gives $(c^2-s^2)M-2icsNM$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{UMU^{-1}=(m\cdot\sigma)\cos\theta+((n\times m)\cdot\sigma)\sin\theta.}
$$
The spin-half <angular momentum> is $S_i=\hbar\sigma_i/2$. The commutators then become $[S_i,S_j]=i\hbar\epsilon_{ijk}S_k$, and $(n\cdot S)^2=\hbar^2I/4$ expresses the two possible directional <spin> values $\pm\hbar/2$. Also $S^2=3\hbar^2I/4$, appropriate to <spin> one half. The exponential represents a spatial rotation on <spin> states.

Choose $n=(0,1,0)$ and initially $\sigma_3|\chi\rangle=|\chi\rangle$. The conjugation formula sends $\sigma_3$ to $\sigma_3\cos\theta+\sigma_1\sin\theta$. Therefore
$$
(\sigma_1\sin\theta+\sigma_3\cos\theta)U|\chi\rangle
=U\sigma_3|\chi\rangle=U|\chi\rangle.
$$
Thus **$U|\chi\rangle$ is <spin> up along $(\sin\theta,0,\cos\theta)$**, as required.

= 33B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33B}

For a normalized trial state in the quadratic-form domain of a <self-adjoint> <Hamiltonian operator> bounded below, its <expectation> of $H$ is at least the ground <energy> $E_0$. Minimizing this <Rayleigh quotient> over a tractable trial family therefore supplies an upper bound.

For the two states, set $a_\pm=(a_1\pm a_2)/\sqrt2$. Their quotient is
$$
\frac{(E+\epsilon)a_+^2+(E-\epsilon)a_-^2}
{(1+s)a_+^2+(1-s)a_-^2}.
$$
The strict assumption $\epsilon<sE$ excludes $|s|=1$, since equality in the overlap Cauchy-Schwarz bound would then force the two states to be proportional and $\epsilon=sE$. Thus $|s|<1$. The two generalized <eigenvalues> are $(E\pm\epsilon)/(1\pm s)$, and their difference has the sign of $\epsilon-sE$. The symmetric trial is the lower one, so
$$
\boxed{E_0\leq\frac{E+\epsilon}{1+s}.}
$$

For the single attractive delta well, the supplied <wavefunction> has <norm> one because $\int\lambda e^{-2\lambda|x|}\,dx=1$. Its <kinetic energy> in quadratic-form notation is $\hbar^2\int|\psi_1'|^2/(2m)=\hbar^2\lambda^2/(2m)$, and its <delta potential> <energy> is $-K|\psi_1(0)|^2=-K\lambda$. With $\lambda=mK/\hbar^2$ this gives
$$
\boxed{E_B=-K\lambda/2.}
$$
Its derivative jump is $\psi_1'(0+)-\psi_1'(0-)=-2\lambda\psi_1(0)$, exactly the delta <boundary condition>, so it is an eigenstate. A decaying <bound state> on the two half-lines must have a common decay rate fixed by this same jump; there is only this one negative-energy state, confirming it is the <ground state>.

For $R>0$, integrate the overlap on the three regions $x<0$, $0<x<R$, $x>R$. Put $a=\lambda R$. The result is
$$
\boxed{s=(1+a)e^{-a},\qquad
E=E_B-K\lambda e^{-2a},\qquad
\epsilon=E_Bs-K\lambda e^{-a}.}
$$
The second equality is the single-well <energy> plus the extra delta <expectation>; the third follows by applying either single-well <eigenvalue> equation in the cross <matrix> element. Moreover $\epsilon-sE=K\lambda e^{-a}[(1+a)e^{-2a}-1]<0$, so the preceding bound applies. Substitution gives
$$
\boxed{E_0(R)\leq E_B\left[1+\frac{2e^{-\lambda R}(1+e^{-\lambda R})}{1+(1+\lambda R)e^{-\lambda R}}\right].}
$$
For widely separated wells, each localized orbital is close to an isolated-well <ground state> and the low-energy subspace is accurately their span; the symmetric combination captures the leading tunneling <energy> lowering. In the limit both exact and trial energies approach $E_B$. This illustrates electronic molecular binding: sharing an orbital between attractive centers can lower its <energy> below the separated-center value. A full equilibrium bond length would also require the repulsion of the positive centers.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= Solution
{parent=34D}

For a reversible change of a simple compressible system with fixed composition, the first law is
$$
\boxed{dE=T\,dS-P\,dV.}
$$
An adiabatic change exchanges no heat. A reversible adiabatic change is isentropic, $dS=0$; adiabatic alone does not imply constant <entropy> for an irreversible process.

Define the <Helmholtz free energy> $F=E-TS$. Then $dF=-S\,dT-P\,dV$. Equality of its mixed partial derivatives gives the <Maxwell relation>
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_T
=\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_V.}
$$
Holding $T$ fixed in the first law and using this relation gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial E}{\partial V}\right)_T
=T\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_V-P.}
$$
For equilibrium radiation, $E=Ve(T)$ and $P=e(T)/3$, so this identity reads $e=Te'/3-e/3$, or $Te'=4e$. Integrating gives
$$
\boxed{e=aT^4,\qquad E=aVT^4.}
$$
Finally in a reversible adiabatic expansion, $dE=-P\,dV$ becomes $4aVT^3dT+aT^4dV=-aT^4dV/3$. Dividing gives $3\,dT/T=-dV/V$, hence **$VT^3$ is constant**. This assumes equilibrium radiation throughout the expansion.

= 35C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= Solution
{parent=35C}

At a chosen event $p$, local inertial coordinates have $g_{ab}(p)=\eta_{ab}$, $\partial_cg_{ab}(p)=0$ and $\Gamma^a{}_{bc}(p)=0$. The metric agrees with Minkowski space to first order and freely falling trajectories are momentarily straight, expressing the local <equivalence principle>. Second derivatives generally remain, recording <curvature> and tidal effects.

Differentiate the <Levi-Civita connection>. At $p$, the terms from derivatives of the inverse metric multiply first derivatives of $g$ and vanish, giving
$$
\partial_a\Gamma^c{}_{bd}
=\frac12\eta^{ce}(\partial_a\partial_bg_{ed}+\partial_a\partial_dg_{eb}-\partial_a\partial_eg_{bd}).
$$
For the convention $R^a{}_{bcd}=\partial_c\Gamma^a{}_{bd}-\partial_d\Gamma^a{}_{bc}+\Gamma\Gamma-\Gamma\Gamma$, the connection products vanish at $p$. Lowering the first index and subtracting these derivatives cancels the $\partial_c\partial_dg_{ab}$ terms, yielding
$$
\boxed{R_{abcd}=\frac12(\partial_b\partial_cg_{ad}+\partial_a\partial_dg_{bc}
-\partial_b\partial_dg_{ac}-\partial_a\partial_cg_{bd}).}
$$
Symmetry of $g$ and commutation of second derivatives show $R_{abcd}=R_{cdab}$ in these coordinates. Both sides are tensors, so equality in an inertial chart at each event implies equality at any point in every chart.

For the specified conformal metric, let $s=L^{-2}\eta_{ef}x^ex^f$. Its expansion is $g_{ab}=\eta_{ab}(1-2s+O(|x|^4))$. Thus the original coordinates are already inertial at zero, and
$$
\partial_c\partial_dg_{ab}(0)=-\frac4{L^2}\eta_{ab}\eta_{cd}.
$$
Substitution gives $R_{abcd}(0)=4(\eta_{ac}\eta_{bd}-\eta_{ad}\eta_{bc})/L^2$. In four dimensions the indicated contraction is
$$
\boxed{R(0)=\frac4{L^2}(4^2-4)=\frac{48}{L^2}.}
$$
The sign is fixed by the Riemann-tensor formula in the question; reversing the Riemann convention would reverse the scalar as well.

= 36E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= Solution
{parent=36E}

In the thin-current approximation, vertical momentum balance is hydrostatic: $p=p_{\rm atm}+\rho g(h-z)$. Neglect inertia and radial viscous derivatives compared with the vertical ones. The radial <Stokes equation> is $\mu u_{zz}=p_r=\rho gh_r$, with no slip at $z=0$ and zero tangential stress $u_z=0$ at $z=h$. Integration gives
$$
\boxed{u(r,z,t)=-\frac{\rho g}{\mu}h_r\left(hz-\frac{z^2}{2}\right).}
$$
Its depth-integrated radial flux is $Q_r=\int_0^hu\,dz=-\rho gh^3h_r/(3\mu)$. Axisymmetric <mass conservation> $h_t+r^{-1}\partial_r(rQ_r)=0$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{h_t=\frac\beta r\partial_r(rh^3h_r),\qquad\beta=\frac{\rho g}{3\mu}.}
$$
The remaining conditions are conserved <volume> $2\pi\int_0^{R(t)}rh\,dr=V$, regularity and zero radial flux at the center, $h(R(t),t)=0$, zero thickness outside the front, and the initially concentrated <volume> at the origin. The advancing edge obeys $R'=\lim_{r\uparrow R}Q_r/h$ when this limit exists. One must not impose a finite zero slope at this compact-support edge.

Put $h=t^aF(rt^{-b})$ with dimensional factors to be restored. Constant <volume> gives $a+2b=0$, while balancing the equation gives $a-1=4a-2b$. Hence $a=-1/4,b=1/8$. In normalized similarity variables $\eta=r/(\beta V^3t)^{1/8}$ and $h=V^{1/4}(\beta t)^{-1/4}F(\eta)$, the equation becomes
$$
-\frac14F-\frac18\eta F'=\frac1\eta(\eta F^3F')'.
$$
Multiplying by $\eta$ and integrating from the regular center yields $\eta F^3F'=-\eta^2F/8$. Where $F>0$, this integrates to $F^3=3(\eta_0^2-\eta^2)/16$. Restoring physical variables gives
$$
\boxed{h(r,t)=\left[\frac3{16\beta t}(R(t)^2-r^2)\right]^{1/3}\quad(0\leq r\leq R(t)),}
$$
with zero thickness outside. Write $h=A(t)(1-r^2/R^2)^{1/3}$. Its <volume> is $3\pi AR^2/4$, so $A=4V/(3\pi R^2)$. Combining this with $A^3=3R^2/(16\beta t)$ gives
$$
\boxed{R(t)=\left(\frac{1024\beta V^3t}{81\pi^3}\right)^{1/8}.}
$$
The center height scales as $t^{-1/4}$, while the front scales as $t^{1/8}$. The ideal profile has a singular slope at the edge; microscopic contact-line physics is excluded from the lubrication model, which applies to the sufficiently spread bulk current.

= 37E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= Solution
{parent=37E}

For a one-dimensional isentropic <perfect gas>, <continuity> and momentum are $D\rho=-\rho u_x$ and $Du=-c^2\rho_x/\rho$, where $D=\partial_t+u\partial_x$ and $c^2=\partial P/\partial\rho$. Since $P=K\rho^\gamma$, differentiation gives
$$
Dc=-\frac{\gamma-1}{2}cu_x,\qquad
Du=-\frac{2c}{\gamma-1}c_x.
$$
Thus applying $D+c\partial_x$ to $u+2(c-c_0)/(\gamma-1)$ cancels both terms:
$$
\boxed{(\partial_t+(u+c)\partial_x)\left[u+\frac{2(c-c_0)}{\gamma-1}\right]=0.}
$$
The complementary invariant $u-2(c-c_0)/(\gamma-1)$ is transported along $dx/dt=u-c$. In the right-running piston <simple wave>, those characteristics carry the undisturbed value zero, giving $c=c_0+(\gamma-1)u/2$.

A right-running characteristic emitted by the piston at time $\tau$ has $u=f\tau$ and speed $u+c=c_0+(\gamma+1)f\tau/2$, constant along that characteristic. It starts at $x=f\tau^2/2$, so
$$
x(t;\tau)=\frac12f\tau^2+\left(c_0+\frac{\gamma+1}{2}f\tau\right)(t-\tau).
$$
Neighboring characteristics first intersect when $\partial x/\partial\tau=0$. This derivative is $\frac{\gamma+1}{2}ft-c_0-\gamma f\tau$, so the envelope time is
$$
t(\tau)=\frac{2c_0}{(\gamma+1)f}+\frac{2\gamma}{\gamma+1}\tau.
$$
For $\gamma>1$, it is smallest at $\tau=0$. Consequently the first gradient catastrophe and ensuing <shock> form at
$$
\boxed{t_* =\frac{2c_0}{(\gamma+1)f},\qquad
x_* =c_0t_* =\frac{2c_0^2}{(\gamma+1)f}.}
$$
This uses the smooth isentropic simple-wave solution up to its first characteristic crossing, not beyond the newly formed <shock>.

= 38A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=38A}

For a finite-dimensional linear operator $A$, the <Krylov subspace> is
$$
\mathcal K_m(A,v)=\operatorname{span}\{v,Av,\ldots,A^{m-1}v\}.
$$
These spaces are nested, so their dimensions are nondecreasing and can rise by at most one at each step. If $v\ne0$, let $k$ be the first index for which $A^kv$ is a <linear combination> of $v,\ldots,A^{k-1}v$. The preceding $k$ vectors are <independent>. Multiplying that dependence by $A$ and repeatedly substituting shows every higher power also lies in their span. Thus
$$
\boxed{d_m=m\ (1\leq m\leq k),\qquad d_m=k\ (m\geq k).}
$$
Such an index exists because the ambient space is finite-dimensional. If $v=0$, the separate answer is $d_m=0$ for every $m$, with $k=0$.

For <Krylov dimension from spectral components>, when $A$ is <diagonalizable>, group the <eigenvector> expansion by distinct <eigenvalues>:
$$
v=u_1+\cdots+u_k,\qquad Au_j=\lambda_ju_j,
$$
where each $u_j$ is its nonzero component in a distinct <eigenspace>. Then $A^rv=\sum_j\lambda_j^ru_j$. The vectors $u_j$ are <independent>, and the <Vandermonde matrix> $(\lambda_j^r)_{0\leq r<k}$ is invertible because the <eigenvalues> are distinct. Hence the first $k$ powers span exactly $\operatorname{span}\{u_j\}$, proving **the stabilized dimension equals the number of distinct <eigenspaces> with nonzero component in $v$**. These $u_j$ themselves are <eigenvectors>, so this is also the minimum number of <eigenvectors> needed to represent $v$ when the <eigenvectors> may be chosen freely.

If the printed phrase instead means the count of nonzero coefficients in an arbitrary fixed eigenbasis, it is false with repeated <eigenvalues>. For example $A=I$ and $v=e_1+e_2$ use two vectors of that <basis> but have $\dim\mathcal K_m=1$; the vector $v$ itself is an <eigenvector>. For a simple <spectrum> there is no distinction. This qualification resolves the claim without assuming an unstated simple <spectrum>.
